    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124473,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 26,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:38:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124474,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 26,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Variably sized, sometimes jagged nests of basaloid tumour cells infiltrate the normal dermal collagen. The tumour has an irregular permeating pattern of invasion at the deep tumour edge {27311865}. Approximately one&nbsp;third of infiltrating BCCs are admixed with a nodular BCC component {16446711}. Perineural invasion is more frequent in this type of BCC {16112353}. There is often overlap with sclerosing/morphoeic and micronodular BCCs, which has led to a proposal to group these three tumour types into one group called &ldquo;infiltrative BCC&rdquo; {34931722}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Differentiation of infiltrative BCC from other types of BCC with infiltrative growth (sclerosing/morphoeic and micronodular BCCs) can be difficult. Infiltrating BCC can also be admixed with nodular and superficial BCCs, but the presence of infiltrative morphology defines the tumour type.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:39:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
